  "title": {
    "en": "Exponential &amp; Logarithmic Functions",
    "ar": null
  },
  "chapterTag": {
    "variant": "category",
    "className": "a2-tag",
    "badgeClassName": "a2-paper",
    "emoji": "📌",
    "category": "Exponential &amp; Logarithmic Functions",
    "weightBadge": "A2",
    "topics": [
      "Laws of logs",
      "Solving \\(e^x=k\\)",
      "Exponential growth/decay"
    ]
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Exponential functions model growth and decay; logarithms are their inverses, and the tool that lets you solve for an exponent. The log laws turn products into sums.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 5.1 — Logarithm",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>\\( \\log_{a} b=c \\) means exactly \\( a^{c}=b \\) (with \\( a&gt;0,\\ a\\neq 1 \\)). So a logarithm <em>is</em> an exponent.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Product",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_a(xy)=\\log_a x+\\log_a y \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quotient",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_a\\!\\tfrac{x}{y}=\\log_a x-\\log_a y \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Power",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_a(x^{k})=k\\log_a x \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Change of base",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\log_a x=\\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\( 2^{x}=16 \\).</p><p>Write 16 as a power of 2: \\( 2^{x}=2^{4} \\):</p>\\[ x=4. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 5.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Condense \\( 2\\log x+\\log y \\) to a single logarithm.</p><p>Power rule then product rule:</p>\\[ 2\\log x+\\log y=\\log\\!\\big(x^{2}y\\big). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve \\(2^{x+1}=5^{x-1}\\), giving your answer to 3 significant figures.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Take logs: \\((x+1)\\ln2=(x-1)\\ln5\\). \\(x\\ln2-x\\ln5=-\\ln5-\\ln2\\). \\(x=\\dfrac{-\\ln10}{\\ln2-\\ln5}=\\dfrac{-\\ln10}{\\ln(2/5)}\\approx2.48\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
